{{smaller|(as seen in any quill pen) and lateral rudder-feathers. See cuts under ''aftershaft'', ''barb'', ''ocellate'', and ''penciling''.}}

{{smaller block|They [barbules] make the vane truly a ''web'': that is, they so connect the barbs together that some little force is required to pull them apart.
{{right|''Coues'', Key to N. A. Birds, p. 84.}}}}
'''7.''' The plexus of very delicate threads or filaments which a spider spins, and which serves as a net to catch flies or other insects for its food; a cobweb; also, a similar substance spun and woven into a sort of fabric by many insects, usually as a covering or protection. See ''bag-worm'', ''web-worm'', and ''tent-caterpillar''.
{{smaller block|<poem>The Comissaries court ’s a spiders ''webbe'',
That doth entangle all the lesser flies.</poem>
{{right|''Times’ Whistle'' (E. E. T. S.), p. 81.}}
<poem>Much like a subtle spider, which doth sit
In middle of her ''web'', which spreadeth wide.</poem>
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Immortal. of Soul, xviii.}}}}
'''8.''' Figuratively, anything carefully contrived and elaborately put together or woven; a plot; a scheme.
{{smaller block|All this is but a ''web'' of the wit; it can work nothing.
{{right|''Bacon'', Praise of Knowledge (ed. 1887).}}
<poem>The Fates at length the blissful ''Web'' have spun.</poem>
{{right|''Congreve'', Birth of the Muse.}}
<poem>O, what a tangled ''web'' we weave
When first we practise to deceive!</poem>
{{right|''Scott'', Marmion, vi. 17.}}
It is one ''web'' of intricate complications between the Emperors of the East and West, the Republic of Venice, the Kings of Hungary, Dalmatia, and Bosnia.
{{right|''E. A. Freeman'', Venice, p. 229.}}}}

