'''web''' (web), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''webbed'', ppr. ''webbing''. [< ME. ''webben'', < AS. ''webban'', weave a web; from the noun.] '''1.''' To cover with or as with a web; envelop.—'''2.''' To connect with a web, as the toes of a bird; render palmate.—{{smaller|'''Webbed fingers''', two or more fingers of the human hand which are united by a band of connecting tissue, either occurring congenitally or as an abnormality, or resulting from cicatrization after burns and other wounds; dactylion. See ''web-fingered'', and ''Didot’s operation'' (under ''operation'').—'''Webbed toes''', a condition affecting the toes of the human foot, abnormally or accidentally, similar to that of webbed fingers. See ''web-footed''.}}

'''webbe<sup>1</sup>†''', ''n.'' [< ME. ''webbe'', a weaver, < AS. ''webba'', a weaver, < ''wefan'', weave: see ''weave''<sup>1</sup>, and cf. ''web''. The ME. noun ''webbe'' survives in the proper name ''Webb''.] A weaver. See ''webber''.
{{smaller block|A ''webbe'', a dyere, and a tapicer.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Gen. Prol. to C. T., l. 362.}}}}

